PATCH 540: four object state-machine methods dispatched with DI live bound the object to a stack-
residue parameter and host-deref'd it.  Latent until patches 538/539 let a weapon resolve a hit.

Both dispatches hand the target exactly ONE argument -- the object's DGROUP NEAR OFFSET in DI:

  c31e (the per-type ACTION dispatch, asm 0xc31e):
      c31e: mov %bx,-0x6427          ; [0x9bd9] = BX
      c322: mov %ax,0x9bdb           ; [0x9bdb] = AX   <- AX is NOT written again before c32c
      c325: push %di
      c326: mov %si,%di              ; DI = the object
      c328: mov (%si),%si
      c32a: shl $1,%si
      c32c: call *-0x1ab0(%si)       ; dispatch, DI (and AX) live

  9e2b's state table (asm 0x9ecd `call cs:[0x9f0f+bx]`), whose 7 entries are
      9f1d 9f66 9fe5 a069 a07c a02a a03f
  (patch 497's comment lists six; the seventh, a03f, is the one AZER4 reaches).

Patch 256 and patch 497 pass that near offset as the single argument, so it lands in param_1.  Ghidra,
however, bound each function's DI varnode to a LATER parameter, which at run time is stack residue --
measured param_3=1280 in a0c8 and param_2 garbage in a03f -- and then dereferenced it as a host
pointer.  Every one of these is a SIGSEGV the moment its state is entered.

  a0c8  the DAMAGE-APPLY method (asm 0xa0c8-0xa134).  Needs BOTH live registers:
        DI  = the object (param_1, per c326 above);
        AX  = the weapon index -- c322 stores it to [0x9bdb] and does not touch AX again before the
              call, so AX at entry IS word[DGROUP:0x9bdb] = DAT_2000_5bdb.  That is an identity the
              asm guarantees, not an inference.
        `a0ce mov $0x9b3f,%bx ; a0d1 add %ax,%bx` then forms the DGROUP near offset into the per-weapon
        damage table that b274 takes (patch 493); Ghidra's `param_1 + -0x64c1` is the same 0x9b3f+ax
        as a sign-flipped host int.
  a03f  entry 6 of the 9e2b table (asm 0xa03f-0xa068).  Same base-loss; also `a045 mov 0x2e(%di),%ax`
        is a WORD read, which Ghidra took as an int.
  a02a  entry 5 (asm 0xa02a-0xa03e).  param_1 IS the object but every field was read through it as a
        host pointer.
  a069  entry 3 (asm 0xa069-0xa07b).  Same, plus `cmpw $0x0,0x23(%di)` is a WORD compare read as 4
        bytes.

Callee conventions are left exactly as they are and each call site is matched to the one its callee
already expects: ba33 and b354 take the object NEAR OFFSET (their bodies do `(uint16_t)param`), while
a097 and 9da1 read their argument as a HOST pointer (9da1's only caller is a02a).

Measured, AZER1..AZER4 self-play, 20000 ticks:

  before:  AZER3 SIGSEGV (a0c8), AZER4 SIGSEGV (a03f)
  after:   AZER3 exit 0 a296=18, AZER4 exit 0 a296=15, AZER1 exit 0 a296=15, AZER2 exit 0 a296=18

NOT fixed here, and deliberately: entries 9f66 and 9fe5 of the same table carry the same base-loss but
also need the 054c/register-threading convention established before they can be reconstructed
honestly.  They are recorded on the board rather than guessed at.

